CLINIC QUEUE BOARD

Clinic Queue Board — a real-world task using only today's concepts: creating lists, indexing, slicing, len(), in, split(), comparisons, if / else, variables, input(), and printing. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A clinic receptionist keeps the morning queue as one line of names. Her board program turns that line into a list and answers the standard questions in one go.

The program must:
- Ask for the queue as one line: names separated by a comma and a space, like Adaeze, Bola, Emeka, Fatima. Turn it into a list with split(", ").
- Print exactly four lines:
  FIRST: Adaeze
  LAST: Fatima
  WAITING: 4
  NEXT TWO CALLED: Adaeze, Bola
- Then ask "Search for which patient: ", and print one line: "Adaeze is number 1 in the queue" (their position, counting from 1) or "Bola is not in the queue". Ignore capital letters when matching (compare in lowercase).

Sample run:

Queue line: Adaeze, Bola, Emeka, Fatima

FIRST: Adaeze
LAST: Fatima
WAITING: 4
NEXT TWO CALLED: Adaeze, Bola
Search for which patient: emeka
Emeka is number 3 in the queue

Edge cases to handle: a queue of exactly one name (first and last are the same, NEXT TWO shows just the one), a search that matches nobody, a search typed in a different case than the queue line, a queue of exactly two names, and checking positions are counted from 1 - never 0.
